---
/*
 * Dog grants (docs/dogs.md, "Grants"). The refusal text below is the one
 * cli_e2e's a_dog_is_refused_outside_the_grants_written_into_shep_toml pins
 * against a real shepherd. Its own page, not a section of dogs.astro, to
 * keep that page inside its prose budget.
 */
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import ReferencePills from "../../components/docs/ReferencePills.astro";
import ShortVersion from "../../components/docs/ShortVersion.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  activeSlug="dog-grants"
>
  <article>
    <h1>Dog grants</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="dog-grants" />
    <p class="lede">
      A dog talks to the shepherd over a connection of its own, and by
      default it may ask for anything the CLI can. Grants list what it may
      ask instead, so an honest dog stays in bounds and what it asks is
      attributable.
    </p>

    <ShortVersion>
      <ul>
        <li><code>shep adopt</code> and the first <code>shep enable</code> list what a dog declares and ask. A yes writes <code>[daemon.dog_grants.&lt;name&gt;] granted = [...]</code> to <code>shep.toml</code>.</li>
        <li>A request outside the list is refused with a <code>forbidden</code> error naming the dog, the request and the permission.</li>
        <li>No entry means full access. An empty list means nothing.</li>
        <li>Grants are not a sandbox.</li>
      </ul>
    </ShortVersion>

    <h2 id="granting">Granting</h2>
    <CodeBlock>{`$ shep adopt ~/.cargo/bin/shep-otel --name otel
otel declares these permissions:
  flock.read (required): reads the flock to report on it
  events:process. (optional): hears when a sheep exits
Grants keep otel's requests in bounds and do not confine it: it runs as you and can do anything you can.
adopt otel and grant it these? [y/N] y

# shep.toml
[daemon.dog_grants.otel]
granted = ["flock.read", "events:process."]`}</CodeBlock>
    <ul>
      <li>A no leaves the dog adopted and disabled with nothing granted. The first <code>shep enable</code> asks again.</li>
      <li><code>--yes</code> grants everything declared. With no terminal and no <code>--yes</code>, nothing changes and the exit is 2.</li>
      <li>A dog with no manifest prompts <code>declares nothing, runs with full access</code>, and a yes records no entry.</li>
      <li>An unknown required permission refuses the command. An unknown optional one is dropped with a warning.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The shepherd reads the list at boot. A name it cannot read stops the
      boot, and <code>shep adopt</code> and <code>shep enable</code> refuse
      it too, so a typo never silently grants nothing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="permissions">Permissions</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Permission</th><th>Lets the dog</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>flock.read</code></td><td>look at the flock and its configuration</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>flock.control</code></td><td>start, stop, restart and signal sheep</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>flock.edit</code></td><td>add and delete sheep and change their configuration</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>logs.read</code></td><td>read the end of a sheep's log</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>logs.manage</code></td><td>reopen and flush logs</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>events:&lt;prefix&gt;</code></td><td>subscribe to topics that begin with the prefix</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>secrets.read:&lt;scope&gt;</code></td><td>read a sheep's dog settings with secrets resolved, in the environment the scope names</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>secrets.write:&lt;namespace&gt;</code></td><td>push values into that namespace</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dogs.control</code></td><td>enable, disable and configure other dogs</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dogs.trigger:&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>send an action to the named dog</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>shepherd.control</code></td><td>stop the shepherd</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A dog always keeps its own <code>dogs.toml</code> section and its
        own sheep settings, whatever it was granted.
      </li>
      <li>
        A subscription is judged on the part of each topic before its first
        glob character: <code>events:process.</code> covers{" "}
        <code>process.*</code> and <code>events:process.exit</code> does not.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="a-refusal">A refusal</h2>
    <p>
      A dog granted only <code>flock.read</code> that asks to stop a sheep
      gets this back:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`dog \`otel\` was refused \`stop\`: it needs \`flock.control\`, which was not granted to it`}</CodeBlock>

    <h2 id="older-shepherds">Older shepherds</h2>
    <p>
      A shepherd older than protocol 12 records grants and enforces nothing.{" "}
      <code>shep adopt</code> and <code>shep enable</code> say so when they
      reach one.
    </p>

    <Callout variant="careful">
      Grants are not a sandbox. A dog runs as you and could open the control
      socket the way the CLI does. They keep an honest dog inside the lines
      you drew and do not contain a hostile one.
    </Callout>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
